//
// THE WALK — plan 22 `B1c.2b`.
//
// Where a walker ENDS UP, given where it is and where it asked to go. `pose.loft`
// answers what the held keys mean; this answers what the world does about it.
//
// ⚠ IT IS A VERBATIM RELOCATION OUT OF `src/editor_server.loft`, and that is the
// safety of the step rather than a note about its history: every body below is the
// server's, moved unchanged, so the comparison is a DIFF and not an argument. Two
// things did change and both are named at their own function — `edges_walk` takes
// the cliff threshold as a parameter instead of calling the server's `cliff_step()`,
// and the bodies are `pub`.
//
// ⚠ WHY IT COULD MOVE AT ALL, WHICH WAS NOT OBVIOUS AND WAS MEASURED. The walk was
// sized as blocked on two things: `ground_under` needs `hex_mesh::terrain_y` and
// `hex_mesh` depends on THIS package (a cycle), and `cliff_edges` was `moros_sim`'s.
// `B1c.2a` paid the second. The first turned out not to apply: **`walk_to` never
// calls `ground_under`.** Only the FALL does, and the fall is `B1c.3`. Everything
// else the walk reaches for — `edge_layer`, `WALL_MAT`, `FENCE_MAT`, `terrain_h` —
// was already this package's, and the rest is `hex_edge`, `hex_field`, `hex_way`,
// `hex_grid` and `hex_voxel`, every one already a dependency.
//
// ⚠ AND THE HEIGHT SCALE IS NOT HERE, DELIBERATELY. The server measures its
// reference in HEIGHT UNITS and passes integers across this seam; `hex_proj`'s
// `HEIGHT_SCALE` is a global that is **wrong on a part world** (`door/slat` is
// authored at 0.125), and `w.w_unit` is the world's own answer. Nothing below needs
// either, because a reference in units is what the surface rule takes.
use self::gesture;
use self::cliff;

use hex_field;
use hex_edge;
use hex_way;
use hex_grid;
use hex_voxel;

// ── COLLISION (rung W2, moros#10) — what the walk cannot cross ─────────────
//
// **The query is `hex_edge`'s; the ANSWER is ours.** `sweep_path` walks a SEGMENT
// across the lattice one edge at a time and stops at the first blocked one, so the
// result is a function of where you are and where you are going — never of how
// many ticks the motion was cut into. A dropped frame cannot tunnel through a
// fence, and there is no step size anywhere to tune.
//
// What belongs here is the PREDICATE, and there are two of them:
//
//   material   stops a walker   stops the camera
//   WALL       yes              yes
//   DOOR       no               no
//   FENCE      yes              **no**
//
// That last row is the whole reason they are two functions rather than one. The
// camera's discriminator is NOT "does it block movement" — a fence stops a
// character and is visually almost nothing, so avoiding it would make the camera
// lurch every time you walked beside a paddock. It is whether the camera can be
// INSIDE the thing, because a volume you are inside fills the entire screen while
// a thing you see past costs a fraction of the frame (`EDITOR_SUBSTRATE.md`).
//
// The library owns the traversal and knows nothing about materials; the consumer
// supplies the answer. Same seam as the opaque palette in the world contract.
pub fn wall_stops_walk(mat: integer) -> boolean { mat == WALL_MAT || mat == FENCE_MAT }
pub fn wall_stops_view(mat: integer) -> boolean { mat == WALL_MAT }

// ── WHAT A WALKER MEASURES ITS STEP AGAINST ─────────────────────────────────
//
// The surface rule, from the walker's own height — `world_surface`, not
// `terrain_h`. The distinction is the whole of `WORLD_MODEL.md` § "Which layer is
// the surface" and it is invisible until a column has more than one: the outdoors
// is ONE layer per column, and a walker on a first floor is measuring its next
// step against a floor three metres under its feet.
//
// ⚠ IT MADE THE UPPER STOREY UNREACHABLE, which is the shape the defect took.
// The cell beside a deck reads as the ground below it, so a stair built level with
// the deck reported a twelve-unit rise and the walker was refused at the top step.
// The deck half of the camera's and the road's surface rules could not be tested
// for exactly this reason — nothing could stand on a deck to test them from.
//
// ⚠ AND THE FALLBACK IS NOT A TIDY-UP. `world_surface` answers −1 where no terrain
// is occupied at all — a missing chunk, an unauthored cell — while `terrain_h`
// answers the outdoors' own 0 there. Mapping −1 to 0 would put a false cliff
// around every chunk the streamer has not reached; deferring to `terrain_h` keeps
// today's answer wherever the new rule has nothing to say.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE REFERENCE IS THE SURFACE THE WALKER STANDS ON, NEVER ITS FEET**, and
// the difference cost this rung a working stair. `py` rides the INTERPOLATED
// heightfield — every corner is the mean of the three cells touching it — so
// approaching a 12-unit drop the feet sink toward `(12 + 0 + 8)/3`, more than five
// units below the cell's own stored height. `world_surface`'s tolerance is `ε/2`,
// four units, and it is documented as absorbing exactly this difference: it does
// not, because the dip is a property of the NEIGHBOURS and nothing bounds it. Fed
// the sagging feet, the rule stopped seeing a deck 12 above them and the last step
// onto it read as a cliff — intermittently, depending on where in the cell the
// proxy happened to be built.
//
// The two cases turn out to be complementary, which is what makes the stored
// height a safe reference rather than a lucky one:
//
//   * where the feet are smoothed, the column has ONE occupied layer (`sf_smooth`)
//     — and then every reference gives the same answer, so it cannot matter;
//   * where the reference matters, the column has SEVERAL — and then the surface is
//     a deck, taken flat, so the feet are exactly its stored height.
//
// So the caller resolves its own cell once (`py` is a fine reference *there*, by
// the first bullet) and passes that integer in for every other cell in the window.
pub fn walk_h(q: integer, r: integer, wld: VoxelWorld, ref_units: float) -> integer {
  u = hex_voxel::world_surface(wld, q, r, ref_units).sf_units;
  if u < 0 { return terrain_h(q, r, wld); }
  u
}

// The walk's collision set, with the ground's own cliffs blocked into it.
//
// ⚠ MOVE ONLY, NEVER VIEW. A cliff stops a walker and does not stop a camera — the
// editor already keeps those two sets apart (`wall_stops_walk` against
// `wall_stops_view`), and putting cliffs in the view set would make the camera
// shove itself away from every ledge.
//
// ⚠ IT TAKES THE WALKER'S OWN SURFACE, so this set is no longer a function of the
// world alone — it is a function of the world AND the level the walker is on.
// Whatever caches it has to say so: see the proxy's key, which gained the surface
// height for this reason and would otherwise hand a walker who just fell off a deck
// the edges of the deck it fell from.
//
// ⚠ THE CLIFF THRESHOLD IS A PARAMETER — plan 22 `B1c.2b`, and it is the library's
// own seam rather than a change of mind. This called the server's `cliff_step()`,
// which is `hip_wu()` over the height scale: *how tall a step a creature can take
// is a property of the CREATURE, not of the world*, and `cliff_edges` has said so
// at its own signature since it was written. The figure's proportions stay with
// the driver that knows which figure is walking.
pub fn edges_walk(wld: VoxelWorld, cq: integer, cr: integer, reach: integer,
              runs: WallRuns, sf: Surfaces, ref_units: float,
              step_max: integer) -> EdgeSet {
  e = edges_around(wld, cq, cr, reach, false, runs, sf, ref_units);
  n = 2 * reach + 1;
  // The terrain goes in as a FUNCTION, which is what makes the derivation pure and
  // testable without a world — the same shape `ground_axle` takes its terrain by.
  hex_editor::cliff_edges(e, fn(sq: integer, sr: integer) -> integer {
                         walk_h(sq, sr, wld, ref_units) },
                    cq - reach, cr - reach, n, n, step_max);
  e
}

// ⚠ IT READS THE WALLS OF THE LEVEL THE OBSERVER IS ON. Wall bytes live on a
// `Hex`, and a `Hex` is per-layer, so a fence rung on a deck is the DECK's fence —
// it must stop a walker up there and must not stop one crossing the yard below.
// This read `world_ground_cell`, so it did the opposite of both.
pub fn edges_around(wld: VoxelWorld, cq: integer, cr: integer, reach: integer,
                view: boolean, runs: WallRuns, sf: Surfaces,
                ref_units: float) -> EdgeSet {
  n = 2 * reach + 1;
  e = edgeset_new(cq - reach, cr - reach, n, n);
  slot_dir: vector<integer> = [4, 5, 0];        // SLOT_NW, SLOT_NE, SLOT_E
  // ⚠ EACH BLOCKED EDGE CARRIES THE SURFACE OF THE RUN THAT PUT IT THERE. That is
  // the whole reason `hex_edge` stores a surface rather than a boolean: a hex has
  // six normals, and against a wall crossing the lattice diagonally its own edge
  // normal is wrong by up to 90° — measured by the library, not assumed here. A
  // slide along that normal would send a walker sideways or backwards.
  //
  // `nearest_seg` is the library's own arbitration — nearest wins, ties to the
  // lower index — the same rule `way_stamp` cuts boundaries with.
  rt = runs_track(runs);
  first = 0;
  if len(runs.items) > 0 { first = hex_way::way_surfaces(rt, sf); }
  for r in (cr - reach - 1)..(cr + reach + 2) {
    for q in (cq - reach - 1)..(cq + reach + 2) {
      // ⚠ `w_unit`, NOT `HEIGHT_SCALE`, AND IT IS THE ONE LINE OF THIS FILE THAT
      // IS NOT VERBATIM. The server multiplied by `hex_proj`'s global; a world
      // carries its own unit, and the global is **wrong on a part world** —
      // `door/slat` is authored at 0.125. They are the same 0.25 in every
      // landscape the editor makes, which is why the gates can confirm this moved
      // nothing, and taking the global here would have meant a new dependency on a
      // number that is only sometimes right.
      c = hex_voxel::world_cell(wld, q, r,
            edge_layer(wld, q, r, ref_units * wld.w_unit));
      mats: vector<integer> = [c.h_wall_nw as integer ?? 0,
                               c.h_wall_ne as integer ?? 0,
                               c.h_wall_e as integer ?? 0];
      for si in 0..3 {
        stops = wall_stops_walk(mats[si]);
        if view { stops = wall_stops_view(mats[si]); }
        if stops {
          (nq, nr) = hex_grid::hex_neighbor(q, r, slot_dir[si]);
          sid = 0;
          if first > 0 {
            // ⚠ `hex_to_px`, NOT `hex_proj::hex_to_world` — plan 22 `B1c.2b`. The
            // server called the latter with a height of 0 and read only `.x` and
            // `.z`, so it was `hex_grid`'s own function wrapped in a `Vec3` and a
            // multiply by a height scale that was always zero. Taking the wrapper
            // would have made `graphics` a dependency of the walk. Same retirement
            // `B1b.2c.1` made of `world_to_hex`, through a different door.
            (awx, awz) = hex_to_px(q, r);
            (bwx, bwz) = hex_to_px(nq, nr);
            mx = (awx + bwx) * 0.5;
            mz = (awz + bwz) * 0.5;
            if hex_way::track_distance(rt, mx, mz) < 0.9 {
              seg = hex_way::nearest_seg(rt, mx, mz);
              if seg >= 0 { sid = first + seg; }
            }
          }
          if sid > 0 { hex_edge::edge_block_surf(e, q, r, nq, nr, sid); }
          else { hex_edge::edge_block(e, q, r, nq, nr); }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  e
}

// Move toward a point, stopping at the first wall in the way.
//
// Stopping DEAD rather than sliding is this rung's answer, and it is deliberate:
// a slide needs the wall's true normal, and a hex edge carries only six of them —
// measured by `hex_edge` as wrong by up to 90° against a wall crossing the lattice
// diagonally. A slide along the wrong normal is worse than a stop, so the surface
// that carries the exact geometry comes first, and the slide after it.
//
// ⚠ IT STOPS A SKIN SHORT OF THE WALL, and that is not a fudge — it is what makes
// the stop hold. `sweep_path` reports the exact fraction at which the segment
// meets the bisector, and standing exactly THERE is standing exactly between two
// cells: `hex_at` has to round, and when it rounds to the far one the next tick
// begins on the other side of the wall with the wall behind it. Measured — the
// character stopped dead on the fence for one tick and walked through on the next,
// which reads as "collision does not work" and is really "collision worked once".
//
// A cell test cannot be made exact at its own boundary, so the position is kept
// unambiguously on the walkable side instead. `WALK_SKIN` is 1 cm at this scale.
//
// ⚠ NAMED `WALK_SKIN` RATHER THAN `SKIN` — plan 22 `B1c.2b`, and a test found it.
// Published as a bare `SKIN` it collided with `tests/boom.loft`'s own `SKIN` (0.20,
// the camera boom's clearance): two different quantities, one generic name, and the
// package would not build. **A public name is the whole consumer's namespace**, so
// what leaves a library says which thing it is.
pub const WALK_SKIN = 0.01;

// Hold the walker a skin clear of every wall's LINE, whichever path the move took.
//
// ⚠ THIS IS THE FIX THE TRACE ASKED FOR, and the reason it is here rather than
// inside the slide: the tick that crossed the fence logged as FREE. The slide had
// already left the walker on the line a tick earlier, and the move that carried
// them over it met no edge at all — so a standoff applied only where a wall was
// hit is applied everywhere except where it was needed.
//
// The distance is to the SEGMENT (`track_distance`), never to the infinite line: a
// walker past the end of a wall must not be pushed off geometry that stops short
// of them. The side is the side they were on before the move — a standoff may not
// decide which side of a wall someone is on, only how far clear they stand.
pub fn stand_clear(rt: Track, x0: float, z0: float, fx: float, fz: float) -> (float, float) {
  seg = hex_way::nearest_seg(rt, fx, fz);
  if seg < 0 { return (fx, fz); }
  // Only a wall the move actually runs BESIDE can hold it: past a wall's end the
  // nearest point is its endpoint, and an infinite line there is geometry that is
  // not present. Distance-to-segment and distance-to-line agree exactly where the
  // nearest point is interior — the same test the fence's own end-clip uses.
  dseg = hex_way::seg_distance(rt, seg, fx, fz);
  (tx2, tz2) = hex_way::seg_tangent(rt, seg, 0.0);
  nx2 = 0.0 - tz2;
  nz2 = tx2;
  (ax2, az2) = hex_way::seg_point(rt, seg, 0.0);
  d1 = nx2 * (fx - ax2) + nz2 * (fz - az2);
  dline = d1; if dline < 0.0 { dline = 0.0 - dline; }
  over = dseg - dline; if over < 0.0 { over = 0.0 - over; }
  if over > 0.000001 { return (fx, fz); }
  // ⚠ A SIDE TEST, NOT A DISTANCE ONE. A skin is 1 cm and a stride is 13, so a
  // walker steps clean over any band a distance test could guard — measured, that
  // is exactly what build 180232 did. What must not change is the SIDE: the wall
  // decides which side you are on, and a move that flips it is a crossing however
  // far from the line it lands.
  d0 = nx2 * (x0 - ax2) + nz2 * (z0 - az2);
  want = WALK_SKIN;
  if d0 < 0.0 { want = 0.0 - WALK_SKIN; }
  if (d0 >= 0.0 && d1 >= want) || (d0 < 0.0 && d1 <= want) { return (fx, fz); }
  push = want - d1;
  (fx + nx2 * push, fz + nz2 * push)
}

pub fn walk_to(coll: EdgeSet, sf: Surfaces, rt: Track, wld: VoxelWorld,
           x0: float, z0: float, x1: float, z1: float,
           ref_units: float) -> (float, float) {
  (t, hq, hr, hd) = hex_edge::sweep_path(coll, x0, z0, x1, z1);
  if hd < 0 { return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, x1, z1); }
  // ── ⚠ A CLIFF STOPS A CLIMB, NOT A DESCENT ───────────────────────────────
  //
  // `hex_edge` blocks the CANONICAL edge, so a cliff cannot say which side is high;
  // the asymmetry is resolved here, where the walker's side is known. A cliff is
  // told from a wall by its surface — `cliff_edges` blocks with `SURF_NONE` while a
  // wall carries its run's real one — so a wall standing ON a cliff edge keeps its
  // surface and still stops you.
  //
  // ⚠ AND IT ASKS THE SAME QUESTION THE BLOCK DID. This read `terrain_h` while the
  // block was derived from `walk_h`, which is one rule stated twice — and off a
  // deck the two disagree by a whole storey, so a step down from a first floor
  // would have read as a climb and been refused. Both sides ask the surface at the
  // walker's own feet.
  cf_nq = nb_q(hq, hr, hd);
  cf_nr = nb_r(hq, hr, hd);
  if edge_surf(coll, hq, hr, cf_nq, cf_nr) == hex_edge::SURF_NONE {
    if walk_h(cf_nq, cf_nr, wld, ref_units) < walk_h(hq, hr, wld, ref_units) {
      return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, x1, z1);
    }
  }
  seg = sqrt((x1 - x0) * (x1 - x0) + (z1 - z0) * (z1 - z0)) ?? 0.0;
  back = t;
  if seg > 0.0 { back = t - (WALK_SKIN / seg ?? 0.0); }
  if back < 0.0 { back = 0.0; }
  sx = x0 + (x1 - x0) * back;
  sz = z0 + (z1 - z0) * back;

  // ── SLIDE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Stopping dead is what rung 6b shipped, and only because the normal was not
  // available: a hex edge carries six of them and the true wall's is not among
  // them. Now the edge carries its RUN's surface, so `collide` returns the exact
  // one and the leftover motion can be projected onto the wall.
  //
  // A degenerate normal is the honest stop: `edge_block` records SURF_NONE for an
  // edge no run claims — the stencil's house, an edge placed by hand — and sliding
  // along a normal that is not recoverable is worse than not sliding.
  (blocked, nx, nz) = hex_edge::collide(coll, sf, hq, hr,
                                        nb_q(hq, hr, hd), nb_r(hq, hr, hd));
  if !blocked { return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, sx, sz); }
  // A degenerate normal is the honest stop: `edge_block` records SURF_NONE for an
  // edge no run claims — the stencil's house, an edge placed by hand — and sliding
  // along a normal that is not recoverable is worse than not sliding.
  if nx * nx + nz * nz < 0.5 { return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, sx, sz); }
  rx = (x1 - x0) * (1.0 - back);
  rz = (z1 - z0) * (1.0 - back);
  dot = rx * nx + rz * nz;
  tx = rx - nx * dot;
  tz = rz - nz * dot;
  if tx * tx + tz * tz < 0.0000001 { return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, sx, sz); }
  // One more sweep, so the slide cannot pass through a SECOND wall — a corner
  // stops the walker rather than squeezing them round it.
  (t2, q2, r2, d2) = hex_edge::sweep_path(coll, sx, sz, sx + tx, sz + tz);
  if d2 < 0 { return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, sx + tx, sz + tz); }

  // ⚠ THE WALL YOU ARE SLIDING ALONG CANNOT STOP YOU SLIDING ALONG IT, and this
  // is the whole difficulty of the slide rather than a detail of it. The geometry
  // is a straight line; the PROXY is the staircase of edges that line crosses. A
  // walker pressed against the wall and moving parallel to it meets the steps of
  // its own staircase within a millimetre, so every slide stopped dead — which
  // looked exactly like no slide at all.
  //
  // The surface is what tells them apart. If the blocking edge belongs to the same
  // wall — its normal parallel to the one we are sliding on — it is a step, not an
  // obstacle, and the tangential move stands. A different wall is a corner and
  // stops us. Nothing here compares positions or invents a tolerance in the
  // lattice: it is one dot product between two exact normals.
  // ⚠ HERE IS WHERE THE SLIDE STOPS, AND WHY IT IS NOT FINISHED.
  //
  // COUNTED, since the last attempt guessed: for a 27.71 wu road the halfplane cut
  // marks 214 edges over the padded window, 128 reach the world, and a radius-8
  // proxy round the walker holds 36-42 of those. Nothing is lost — the window runs
  // ~45 wu along the line against a 27.71 wu run, and 214 x 27.71/45 = 132 against
  // 128 observed. The fence in the world is COMPLETE and dense: 4.6 edges per world
  // unit, one every 0.217 wu, which no walker steps over.
  //
  // So the earlier suspicion — that the world holds fewer edges than the cut does —
  // is REFUTED, and with it the explanation for the 40° crossing. That crossing
  // needs re-measuring against a build that is certainly the one under test; the
  // two runs it was seen in returned identical trails to the pixel, which is not
  // what two different builds of a float simulation do.
  //
  // What IS established, by probe rather than by reading: the proxy carries the
  // runs' surfaces (158 edges on a fenced road), and `collide` returns the exact
  // normal at the hit — `(0,1)` against an east-west road, which is the run's own
  // and not one of a hex's six. The tangent is therefore right, and the slide code
  // below runs. So the earlier note in this file, that the degenerate-normal bail
  // was being taken, was simply wrong.
  //
  // What defeats it is the PROXY, not the geometry. The wall is a line; the thing
  // swept against is the staircase of edges that line crosses. A walker pressed
  // against the wall and moving parallel to it meets the steps of its OWN
  // staircase within a millimetre, so the second sweep blocks at t ≈ 0 and the
  // slide is a dead stop.
  //
  // Waving those steps through — "the blocking edge belongs to the wall I am
  // sliding along, so it is a step and not an obstacle" — was tried and is WRONG:
  // measured, a walker at 40° then crossed the fence outright, and adding a
  // which-side-of-the-line guard did not stop it, because the crossing happens on
  // a tick where the sweep does not block at all. That last part is unexplained
  // and is the thread to pull next: a cut is supposed to separate in all 24
  // headings BY CONSTRUCTION, so either the world holds fewer edges than the cut
  // does — the end-clip in `road_stamp` is the suspect — or the proxy drops some
  // when it copies them.
  //
  // ── WHY THE SWEEP "MISSES" THAT TICK — traced, and it does not miss ────────
  //
  // Tick by tick, with a build id on the wire to prove which code answered:
  //
  //   free          (2.697, 2.263) -> (2.779, 2.332)
  //   slide-through (2.779, 2.332) -> (2.861, 2.363)   n = (0,1)
  //   free          (2.861, 2.363) -> (3.024, 2.5003)  <- the wall is at 2.500
  //
  // The last segment ENDS 0.0003 past the wall, and `sweep_path` returns free for
  // a crossing at t > 1 BY DESIGN. So nothing is missed and no edge is absent: the
  // walker was left ON the line, and the tick after starts on its far side — the
  // same rounding ambiguity `WALK_SKIN` exists to prevent.
  //
  // The cause is the WALK_SKIN's FRAME OF REFERENCE. It stands the walker off the
  // STAIRCASE EDGE that was hit; a slide then runs along the wall to where the
  // staircase and the analytic line are half a cell apart, and the standoff stops
  // meaning anything. A standoff has to be measured from the SURFACE.
  //
  // ⚠ That remedy was attempted — push the slid position back to a skin from the
  // line, via `edge_point` and the normal — and it did NOT hold: build 178773
  // crossed on the same trail. So the DIAGNOSIS is measured and the REMEDY is not,
  // and after three wrong causes in this rung the difference is stated rather than
  // blurred. hexbody's rule (`ROUNDTRIP.md`): a fact without a gate is a proposal.
  //
  // Until the remedy is gated the walker stops. A stop is honest; a wall you can
  // walk through at 40° is not.
  // The wall you are sliding along cannot stop you sliding along it: its own
  // staircase steps sit a millimetre away and blocked every slide dead. The
  // surface tells a step from an obstacle — one dot product between two exact
  // normals — and `stand_clear` is what makes waving it through safe, since the
  // walker can no longer end up on the line whatever this branch returns.
  (b2ok, n2x, n2z) = hex_edge::collide(coll, sf, q2, r2,
                                       nb_q(q2, r2, d2), nb_r(q2, r2, d2));
  if b2ok {
    par = n2x * nx + n2z * nz;
    if par < 0.0 { par = 0.0 - par; }
    if par > 0.9 { return stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, sx + tx, sz + tz); }
  }
  seg2 = sqrt(tx * tx + tz * tz) ?? 0.0;
  b2 = t2;
  if seg2 > 0.0 { b2 = t2 - (WALK_SKIN / seg2 ?? 0.0); }
  if b2 < 0.0 { b2 = 0.0; }
  stand_clear(rt, x0, z0, sx + tx * b2, sz + tz * b2)
}
